<!-- WHY AEROSOL CAN MATERIAL SELECTION MATTERS -->
<section class="section" id="why-aerosol-can-material-selection-matters">
  <h2>Why Aerosol Can Material Selection Matters</h2>
  <p>Aerosol cans are pressure vessels that must safely contain liquefied or compressed propellants, maintain formulation stability for years, and remain compatible with automated high-speed filling lines. The choice of can material directly influences pressure tolerance, internal coating requirements, transport economics, and recyclability. Material and construction requirements are addressed in technical guidance published by the <a href="https://aerosol.org">European Aerosol Federation (FEA)</a>.</p>
                <p>An incorrect material choice can lead to corrosion failures, deformation, valve leakage, or regulatory non-compliance—issues that often surface only after products reach the market, when remediation is costly. See our foundational <a href="/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide/">Aerosol Manufacturing 101 guide</a> for how material selection fits into the broader formulation and safety system.</p>

                <h3>Key implications of can material selection include:</h3>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Factor</th>
                                <th>Implication</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
