</section>

<!-- WHAT ARE ALUMINIUM AEROSOL CANS? -->
<section class="section" id="what-are-aluminium-aerosol-cans">
  <h2>What Are Aluminium Aerosol Cans?</h2>
  <p>Aluminium aerosol cans are manufactured as seamless, single-piece containers using the impact extrusion process. A slug of aluminium is cold-formed into a cylindrical can body with an integral dome, eliminating side seams entirely. This seamless construction provides uniform wall thickness and excellent surface finish.</p>
                <p>Aluminium cans are widely used in cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, food aerosols, and premium personal care products, where aesthetics, weight reduction, and corrosion resistance are critical. Can material selection is one part of the broader discipline covered in our <a href="/aerosol-technology-and-formulations">aerosol technology and formulations</a> resource.</p>

                <h3>Key technical characteristics of aluminium aerosol cans:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Seamless one-piece construction with no welded joints</li>
                    <li>Naturally corrosion-resistant base metal</li>
                    <li>Lightweight structure reduces transport costs</li>
                    <li>Requires internal organic coatings for most formulations</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="what-are-tinplate-cans">
</section>

<!-- WHAT ARE TINPLATE AEROSOL CANS? -->
<section class="section" id="what-are-tinplate-aerosol-cans">
  <h2>What Are Tinplate Aerosol Cans?</h2>
  <p>Tinplate aerosol cans are made from tin-coated low-carbon steel sheets, formed into cylindrical bodies through deep drawing or three-piece welded construction. The base and dome are attached separately using mechanical seaming, resulting in a multi-component structure.</p>
                <p>Tinplate cans dominate automotive, industrial, household, paint, and insecticide aerosols, where mechanical strength, high-pressure tolerance, and cost efficiency are prioritized over aesthetics. See our step-by-step breakdown of <a href="/how-aerosol-cans-are-manufatured/">how aerosol cans are manufactured</a> for the full impact-extrusion and sheet-forming processes behind both can types.</p>

