<!-- MANUFACTURING PROCESS COMPARISON -->
<section class="section" id="manufacturing-process-comparison">
  <h2>Manufacturing Process Comparison</h2>
  <p>The manufacturing route defines not only the physical properties of the can but also its cost structure and quality control complexity. Aluminium and tinplate cans follow fundamentally different forming technologies, each with specific engineering trade-offs.</p>
                <p>Aluminium impact extrusion requires high-tonnage presses and precise slug metallurgy, while tinplate can production relies on sheet forming, welding, and multiple seaming operations. Seam and joint count directly affects leak risk on the filling line — see our walkthrough of <a href="/aerosol-crimping-gassing-leak-detection-explained/">crimping, gassing, and leak detection</a> for how these interfaces are tested before dispatch.</p>

                <h3>Manufacturing Differences in Summary:</h3>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Aluminium Cans</th>
                                <th>Tinplate Cans</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Process Steps</strong></td>
                                <td>Impact extrusion, ironing, trimming, washing, coating, curing</td>
                                <td>Sheet cutting, body forming, welding, flanging, seaming</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Joints</strong></td>
                                <td>Fewer joints (seamless)</td>
                                <td>Multiple joints (weld + seams)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Leak Risk</strong></td>
                                <td>Lower leak-risk interfaces</td>
                                <td>Higher leak-risk due to seams</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Rigidity</strong></td>
                                <td>Ductile, lower rigidity</td>
                                <td>Higher dimensional rigidity</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="mechanical-strength">
</section>

<!-- MECHANICAL STRENGTH AND PRESSURE PERFORMANCE -->
<section class="section" id="mechanical-strength-and-pressure-performance">
  <h2>Mechanical Strength and Pressure Performance</h2>
  <p>Pressure resistance is a critical safety parameter governed by international aerosol standards. While both materials meet regulatory requirements when properly specified, their mechanical behavior differs under stress. Burst and pressure-cycle testing follows standardized methods maintained by <a href="https://www.astm.org">ASTM International</a>. See our guide to <a href="/aerosol-can-safety-explained/">aerosol can safety and burst testing</a> for how these pressure thresholds and DOT compliance are calculated.</p>
                <p>Tinplate steel has higher yield strength and resists deformation more effectively at high internal pressures. Aluminium, while ductile, relies on precise wall thickness control and dome geometry for pressure resistance.</p>

                <figure>
                    
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/hydrostatic-burst-test-machine.jpg-ETfIt8tTERAR09th.png" 
          alt="Hydrostatic burst pressure testing machine for aerosol cans — Global Aerosols"
          width="1200" 
          height="800" 
          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>
      
                    <figcaption>Figure 3: Burst pressure testing is essential to validate can strength.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <h3>Performance characteristics:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Tinplate cans tolerate higher internal pressures with lower deformation</li>
                    <li>Aluminium cans deform plastically before rupture, offering visible warning signs</li>
                    <li>Both materials require compliance with pressure and burst testing standards</li>
                    <li>Final performance depends on thickness, dome design, and quality control</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="corrosion-behavior">
</section>

<!-- CORROSION BEHAVIOR AND INTERNAL COATINGS -->
<section class="section" id="corrosion-behavior-and-internal-coatings">
  <h2>Corrosion Behavior and Internal Coatings</h2>
  <p>Neither aluminium nor tinplate aerosol cans are universally corrosion-proof without coatings. Internal corrosion is influenced by formulation pH, water content, solvent polarity, and propellant chemistry.</p>
                <p>Aluminium forms a natural <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passivation_(chemistry)">passivating oxide layer</a> that provides baseline corrosion resistance, but aggressive formulations still require epoxy, polyester, or BPA-NI coatings. Tinplate steel is inherently susceptible to corrosion and always requires a protective internal lacquer. See our two-part breakdown of <a href="/aerosol-can-corrosion-1/">aerosol can corrosion causes and control</a> for the electrochemical mechanisms behind these failure modes.</p>

